<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-3d-computer-graphics">
  <title>1.5 3D Computer Graphics</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Results from the previous section on 2D graphics have a natural extension
      to three dimensions. In this section we extend the data matrix and
      homogeneous coordinates to three dimensions. This will allow us to model
      translations and composite transforms involving many points with matrix
      multiplication.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-rotations-in-3d">
    <title>Rotations in 3D</title>

    <p>
      Rotations about the origin are linear transforms. Because they are linear
      they can be expressed in the form <m>T(\vec x) = A\vec x</m> where
      <m>A</m> is a <m>3\times 3</m> matrix, and we can obtain the columns of
      matrix <m>A</m> by transforming the standard vectors
      <md>
        \vec e_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1\\0\\0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad
        \vec e_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0\\1\\0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad
        \vec e_3 = \begin{pmatrix} 0\\0\\1 \end{pmatrix}
      </md>
      We will use the convention that a positive rotation is in the
      counterclockwise direction when looking toward the origin from the
      positive half of the axis of rotation. For example, rotating
      <m>\vec e_1</m> about the <m>x_3</m>-axis by <m>\theta</m> radians
      results in the vector
      <md>
        T(\vec e_1) =
        \begin{pmatrix} \cos\theta\\\sin\theta\\0 \end{pmatrix}
      </md>
      Transforming the first standard vector <m>\vec e_1</m> yields the first
      column of <m>A</m>. Likewise the remaining columns can be found by
      transforming the other standard vectors.
      <md>
        T(\vec e_2) =
        \begin{pmatrix} - \sin\theta\\\cos\theta\\0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad
        T(\vec e_3) = \begin{pmatrix} 0\\0\\1 \end{pmatrix}
      </md>
      The third standard vector does not change under this transformation
      because it is parallel to the rotation axis. The standard matrix for a
      rotation about the <m>x_3</m>-axis is
      <md>
        A = \begin{pmatrix}
              T(\vec e_1) \amp T(\vec e_2) \amp T(\vec e_3)
            \end{pmatrix}
          = \begin{pmatrix}
              \cos \theta \amp -\sin\theta \amp 0 \\
              \sin\theta  \amp \cos\theta  \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
      </md>
      A similar analysis gives us the standard matrices for rotations about the
      <m>x_1</m> and the <m>x_2</m> axes. Results are summarized in
      <xref ref="tab-3d-rotations"/>. The standard matrices in the table can be
      multiplied together to model transforms that perform multiple
      transformations. The next example demonstrates this application.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-3d-rotations">
      <title>
        Standard matrices for 3D rotations about the coordinate axes.
      </title>
      <tabular>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>rotation axis</cell>
          <cell>standard matrix</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>x_1</m>-axis</cell>
          <cell>
            <m>
              \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp \cos\theta \amp -\sin\theta \\
                0 \amp \sin\theta \amp \cos\theta
              \end{pmatrix}
            </m>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>x_2</m>-axis</cell>
          <cell>
            <m>
              \begin{pmatrix}
                \cos \theta \amp 0 \amp -\sin\theta \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                \sin\theta \amp 0 \amp \cos\theta
              \end{pmatrix}
            </m>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>x_3</m>-axis</cell>
          <cell>
            <m>
              \begin{pmatrix}
                \cos \theta \amp -\sin\theta \amp 0 \\
                \sin\theta \amp \cos\theta \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1
              \end{pmatrix}
            </m>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-eg1-3d-rotations">
    <title>Example 1: 3D Rotations</title>

    <p>
      Suppose that the transform <m>\vec{x}\to A\vec x</m> first rotates points
      in <m>\R^3</m> about the <m>x_2</m>-axis by <m>\pi/2</m> radians
      and then rotates points about the <m>x_1</m>-axis by <m>\pi</m> radians.
      We can determine the standard matrix, <m>A</m>, for this transform in a
      few different ways. One approach is to use the standard matrices in
      <xref ref="tab-3d-rotations"/>. The standard matrix, <m>A</m>, is the
      product of two rotation matrices.
      <md>
        <mrow>
          A \amp =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp \cos \pi \amp -\sin\pi \\
            0 \amp \sin\pi \amp \cos\pi
          \end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
            \cos (\pi/2) \amp 0 \amp -\sin(\pi/2) \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
            \sin(\pi/2) \amp 0 \amp \cos(\pi/2)
          \end{pmatrix}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
            0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
            -1 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{pmatrix}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Note that the rotation about the <m>x_2</m>-axis is applied before the
      rotation about the <m>x_1</m>-axis, which determines the multiplication
      order. The standard matrix for the first transformation is placed in the
      rightmost position.
    </p>

    <p>
      We could also obtain the same result by transforming the standard
      vectors, because
      <m>
        A = \begin{pmatrix}
              T(\vec e_1) \amp T(\vec e_2) \amp T(\vec e_3)
            \end{pmatrix}
      </m>.
      The first standard vector gives us the first column of <m>A</m>.
      <md>
        \vec e_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1\\0\\0 \end{pmatrix}
        \to \begin{pmatrix} 0\\0\\1 \end{pmatrix}
        \to \begin{pmatrix} 0\\0\\-1 \end{pmatrix}
      </md>
      This result agrees with our result obtained above by multiplying rotation
      matrices together. Note also that our convention is that a positive
      rotation is in the counterclockwise direction when looking toward the
      origin from the positive half of the axis of rotation.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-data-matrix-3d">
    <title>The Data Matrix for 3D Transforms</title>

    <p>
      Similar to the 2D case, for problems involving many points it is
      convenient to represent the points a data matrix. Analogous to our
      approach in 2D, points in <m>\R^3</m> can be represented in a
      matrix whose columns are vectors that correspond to the points we wish to
      transform. We may transform this matrix with a matrix-vector
      multiplication. Recall that the product of two matrices <m>A</m> and
      <m>D</m>, is defined as
      <md>
        AD = A\begin{pmatrix}
          \vec d_1 \amp \vec d_2 \amp \cdots \amp \vec d_p
        \end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix}
          A\vec d_1 \amp A\vec d_2 \amp \cdots \amp A\vec d_p
        \end{pmatrix}
      </md>
      where <m>\vec d_1, \vec d_2, \cdots , \vec d_p</m> are the columns of
      <m>D</m>. In other words, can perform the transformation on our data by
      computing <m>AD</m>, which transforms each column independently of the
      others. The following example demonstrates this approach.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-eg2-projection-3d">
    <title>Example 2: A Projection in 3D with the Data Matrix</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-cube">
      <title>Corners of a cube with side length 1.</title>
      <tabular>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>x_1</m></cell>
          <cell><m>x_2</m></cell>
          <cell><m>x_3</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Data in Table (<xref ref="tab-cube" text="global"/>) define a cube in
      <m>\R^3</m> with side length 1. Suppose the linear transform
      <m>T(\vec x)</m> projects points in <m>\R^3</m> onto the
      <m>x_1x_2</m>-plane. In this example we will construct the matrix,
      <m>A</m>, that is the standard matrix of the transformation
      <m>T(\vec x) = A \vec x</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The data in Table (<xref ref="tab-cube" text="global"/>) (blue) and its
      projection (green) are shown Figure
      (<xref ref="tab-cube" text="global"/>).
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-cube-projection">
      <caption>
        Data from Table (<xref ref="tab-cube" text="global"/>) and its
        projection onto the <m>x_1x_2</m>-plane.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="img-cube-proj" width="60%">
        <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[
\definecolor{DarkBlue}{HTML}{00008B}
\definecolor{DarkGreen}{HTML}{006400}

\tdplotsetmaincoords{75}{130}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1,
    axis/.style={->,black,-stealth,very thick},
    dsline/.style={black,dashed,thin},
    tdplot_main_coords
    ]

    \coordinate (O) at (0,0,0);

    \filldraw[draw=DarkGreen!40,fill=DarkGreen!08,] (2,2,0) -- (1,2,0) -- (1,1,0) -- (2,1,0) -- cycle;

    \draw[axis] (O) -- (4,0,0) node[below]{$x_1$};
    \draw[axis] (O) -- (0,4,0) node[right]{$x_2$};
    \draw[axis] (O) -- (0,0,3) node[left]{$x_3$};
    \draw[dsline] (1,1,0) -- (1,1,1) node[right]{};
    \draw[dsline] (2,2,0) -- (2,2,1) node[right]{};
    \draw[dsline] (2,1,0) -- (2,1,1) node[right]{};
    \draw[dsline] (1,2,0) -- (1,2,1) node[right]{};

    \filldraw[draw=DarkBlue!80,fill=DarkBlue!08, opacity=0.4] (1,1,1) -- (1,2,1) -- (2,2,1) -- (2,1,1) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkBlue!80,fill=DarkBlue!08, opacity=0.4] (1,1,2) -- (1,2,2) -- (2,2,2) -- (2,1,2) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkBlue!80,fill=DarkBlue!08, opacity=0.4] (1,1,2) -- (1,2,2) -- (1,2,1) -- (1,1,1) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkBlue!80,fill=DarkBlue!08, opacity=0.4] (2,2,2) -- (1,2,2) -- (1,2,1) -- (2,2,1) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkBlue!80,fill=DarkBlue!08, opacity=0.4] (2,2,2) -- (2,1,2) -- (2,1,1) -- (2,2,1) -- cycle;

    \foreach \x/\y/\z in  {1/1/1, 2/1/1, 2/2/1,1/2/1,1/1/2, 2/1/2, 2/2/2,1/2/2 }  \filldraw[DarkBlue] (\x,\y,\z) circle (.1em);

    \foreach \x/\y/\z in  {1/1/0, 2/1/0, 2/2/0,1/2/0 }  \filldraw[DarkGreen] (\x,\y,\z) circle (.1em);

\end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Because the given transform that we are dealing with in this example is
      linear, we can express the transform in the form of a matrix-vector
      product
      <md>
        T(\vec x) = A\vec x
      </md>
      where <m>A</m> is a <m>3\times3</m> matrix. Moreover, because we are
      working with a linear transform, each column of <m>A</m> is equal to the
      product
      <md>
        A\vec e_i, \quad i = 1,2,3
      </md>
      and <m>\vec e_i</m> is a standard vector. For example, the first column
      of <m>A</m> can be found using <m>\vec e_1</m>, which is the vector
      <md>
        \vec e_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1\\0\\0 \end{pmatrix}
      </md>
      Projecting <m>\vec e_1</m> onto the <m>x_1x_2</m>-plane does not change
      the vector, because the vector is already in that plane.
      <md>
        \vec e_1 \to A\vec e_1 = \vec e_1 = \text{first column of } A
      </md>
      The first column of <m>A</m> is <m>\vec e_1</m>. Likewise, the second
      column of <m>A</m> is <m>\vec e_2</m>, becuase <m>\vec e_2</m> is also
      already in the <m>x_1x_2</m>-plane.
      <md>
        \vec e_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0\\1\\0 \end{pmatrix}
        \to A\vec e_2 = \vec e_2 = \text{second column of } A
      </md>
      The last column of <m>A</m> is the projection of <m>\vec e_3</m> onto the
      plane, which is the zero vector.
      <md>
        \vec e_3 = \begin{pmatrix} 0\\0\\1 \end{pmatrix}
        \to A\vec e_3 = \begin{pmatrix} 0\\0\\0 \end{pmatrix}
        = \text{third column of } A
      </md>
      Combining our results for each column of <m>A</m> gives us the standard
      matrix.
      <md>
        A = \begin{pmatrix}
          1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
        \end{pmatrix}
      </md>
      Now that we have the standard matrix for this transform, we can use it to
      transform the data in Table 1. Representing each point as a vector in
      <m>\R^3</m> and placing the vectors in a data matrix, <m>D</m>,
      will allow us to compute the projection using a matrix multiplication.
      Our matrix <m>D</m> is
      <md>
        D = \begin{pmatrix}
          1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\
          1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
          1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 2
        \end{pmatrix}
      </md>
      The transformed points can be computed as follows.
      <md>
        <mrow>
          AD \amp =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\
            1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
            1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 2
          \end{pmatrix}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\
            1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{pmatrix}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Extracting the columns of the product gives us the projected points.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-3d-homogeneous-coordinates">
    <title>3D Homogeneous Coordinates</title>

    <p>
      Homogeneous coordinates in 3D are analogous to the homogeneous 2D
      coordinates we introduced in the previous section.
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="asm-homogeneous-coordinates-3d">
      <title>Homogeneous Coordinates in <m>\R^3</m></title>
      <p>
        <m>(X,Y,Z,1)</m> are homogeneous coordinates for <m>(x,y,z)</m> in
        <m>\R^3</m>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      A translation of the form <m>(x,y,z) \to (x+h,y+k,z+l)</m> can be
      represented as a matrix multiplication with homogeneous coordinates:
      <md>
        \begin{pmatrix}
          1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp h \\
          0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp k \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp l \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
        \end{pmatrix}
        \begin{pmatrix} x\\y\\z\\1 \end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix} x+h\\y+k\\z+l\\1 \end{pmatrix}
      </md>
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-eg3-translation-3d">
      <title>Example 3: A Translation in 3D</title>

      <p>
        The data in Table (<xref ref="tab-cube" text="global"/>) can be
        translated using a homogeneous coordinate system. The data matrix
        <m>D_n</m> in homogeneous coordinates would be
        <md>
          D_h = \begin{pmatrix}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\
            1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
            1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\
            1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix}
        </md>
        The transform that, for example, shifts the data by <m>-3</m> units in
        the <m>x_2</m> direction and by 1 unit in the <m>x_3</m>-direction is
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} D_h \amp =
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp \begin{pmatrix}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\
              -2 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp -2 \amp -2 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp -2 \\
              2 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 3 \amp 3 \amp 3 \\
              1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
          </mrow>
        </md>
        The figure below shows the original data (blue) and its translated
        version (green).
      </p>

      <image xml:id="img-cube-shift" width="60%">
        <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[
\definecolor{DarkBlue}{HTML}{00008B}
\definecolor{DarkGreen}{HTML}{006400}

\tdplotsetmaincoords{75}{110}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1,
    axis/.style={->,black,-stealth,very thick},
    dsline/.style={black,dashed,thin},
    tdplot_main_coords
    ]

    \coordinate (O) at (0,0,0);

    \draw[axis] (-4,0,0) -- (4,0,0) node[below]{$x_1$};
    \draw[axis] (0,-4,0) -- (0,4,0) node[right]{$x_2$};
    \draw[axis] (0,0,-1) -- (0,0,3) node[left]{$x_3$};

    \filldraw[draw=DarkBlue!80,fill=DarkBlue!08, opacity=0.4] (1,1,1) -- (1,2,1) -- (2,2,1) -- (2,1,1) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkBlue!80,fill=DarkBlue!08, opacity=0.4] (1,1,2) -- (1,2,2) -- (2,2,2) -- (2,1,2) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkBlue!80,fill=DarkBlue!08, opacity=0.4] (1,1,2) -- (1,2,2) -- (1,2,1) -- (1,1,1) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkBlue!80,fill=DarkBlue!08, opacity=0.4] (2,2,2) -- (1,2,2) -- (1,2,1) -- (2,2,1) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkBlue!80,fill=DarkBlue!08, opacity=0.4] (2,2,2) -- (2,1,2) -- (2,1,1) -- (2,2,1) -- cycle;

    \foreach \x/\y/\z in  {1/1/1, 2/1/1, 2/2/1,1/2/1,1/1/2, 2/1/2, 2/2/2,1/2/2 }  \filldraw[DarkBlue] (\x,\y,\z) circle (.1em);

    \filldraw[draw=DarkGreen!80,fill=DarkGreen!08, opacity=0.4] (1,-2,2) -- (1,-1,2) -- (2,-1,2) -- (2,-2,2) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkGreen!80,fill=DarkGreen!08, opacity=0.4] (1,-2,3) -- (1,-1,3) -- (2,-1,3) -- (2,-2,3) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkGreen!80,fill=DarkGreen!08, opacity=0.4] (1,-2,3) -- (1,-1,3) -- (1,-1,2) -- (1,-2,2) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkGreen!80,fill=DarkGreen!08, opacity=0.4] (2,-1,3) -- (1,-1,3) -- (1,-1,2) -- (2,-1,2) -- cycle;
    \filldraw[draw=DarkGreen!80,fill=DarkGreen!08, opacity=0.4] (2,-1,3) -- (2,-2,3) -- (2,-2,2) -- (2,-1,2) -- cycle;

    \foreach \x/\y/\z in  {1/-2/2, 2/-2/2, 2/-1/2,1/-1/2,1/-2/3, 2/-2/3, 2/-1/3,1/-1/3 }  \filldraw[DarkGreen] (\x,\y,\z) circle (.1em);

\end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-3d-computer-graphics">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-3d-standard-matrices">
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Construct the standard matrices for the following transforms.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="ex-3d-reflect-plane-x3">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <m>4\times4</m> standard matrix of the transform
            <m>\vec{x}\to A\vec x</m> that uses homogeneous coordinates to
            reflect points in <m>\R^3</m> across the plane
            <m>x_3=k</m>, where <m>k</m> is any real number.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-3d-reflect-plane-sum">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <m>3\times3</m> standard matrix of the transform
            <m>\vec{x}\to A\vec x</m> that reflects points in
            <m>\R^3</m> across the plane <m>x_1 + x_2 = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-3d-rotate-then-project">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <m>3\times3</m> standard matrix of the transform
            <m>\vec{x}\to A\vec x</m> that first rotates points in
            <m>\R^3</m> about the <m>x_3</m>-axis by an angle
            <m>\theta</m> and then projects them onto the
            <m>x_2x_3</m>-plane.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-3d-rotate-about-line">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Line <m>L</m> passes through the point <m>(1,0,0)</m> and is parallel
          to the vector <m>\vec v</m>, where
          <md>
            \vec v = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
          </md>
          Construct the <m>4\times4</m> matrix that uses homogeneous
          coordinates to rotate points in <m>\R^3</m> about line
          <m>L</m> by an angle <m>\theta</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
